course: technology
lesson: 17
title: Inside a car: engine, gears and the dashboard
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A petrol or diesel car is moved by many tiny explosions: fuel burns inside small cylinders, pushes pistons down, and the pistons turn a shaft that turns the wheels.
explain: Gears are like the gears on a bike: low gears give a strong push to start and climb hills, high gears let the wheels turn fast while the engine turns slowly.
explain: The dashboard is the car talking to the driver: sensors check the oil, brakes and tyres and light up a little picture if something is wrong.
explain: Think of a car journey with your family: the engine hums, the indicator ticks before a turn, the wipers swish, and the tyres grip the wet road through their grooves.
explain: Engines and exhausts get very hot and cars are heavy, so children stay away from under the bonnet, from behind a running car and from under a raised car.
fact: In a petrol or diesel engine, fuel burns in small cylinders and pushes pistons, which turn a crankshaft and then the wheels, with many small explosions every second.
fact: Low gears give more turning force to start and climb; high gears let the wheels turn fast while the engine turns slowly; electric cars often need only one gear, because their motors work well at all speeds.
fact: Burnt gases leave through the exhaust pipe, and a catalytic converter changes some poisonous gases into less harmful ones; exhaust fumes include invisible carbon monoxide; burning fuel also makes water vapour, which can cool to drips at the exhaust pipe on a cold morning.
fact: Never stand behind a car with its engine running, and never run an engine in a closed garage.
fact: A petrol station keeps its fuel in big tanks underground that can hold tens of thousands of litres, and pumps measure it into the car.
fact: At the pump there is no smoking and no phone use, and children stay in the car or away from the pumps.
fact: Dashboard warning lights all come on briefly when the car starts, to show the bulbs work, and then go off unless something is wrong.
fact: Turning the steering wheel moves a rod and gears that turn the front wheels, and power steering helps with a motor or hydraulics (oil pushed through pipes).
fact: Indicators flash to show which way a car will turn, and reversing lights are white so people behind know the car is about to move back.
fact: Mechanics lift cars on ramps, plug in computers to read faults and replace worn parts; nobody goes under a raised car except the mechanic doing the job safely.
fact: In the UK, cars older than three years need a yearly test called the MOT.
fact: Some cars have a rain sensor that shines invisible light at the windscreen and switches the wipers on when raindrops change the reflection.
fact: The grooves in a tyre's tread push water away so the rubber can grip; the UK minimum tread depth for cars is 1.6 millimetres, and on wet roads cars need much longer to stop.
exercise: What makes a petrol car move when the driver presses the pedal? | Fuel burns in cylinders, pushing pistons that turn the wheels | A big spring unwinds under the seat | Air from the tyres pushes it forward | A battery heats the road
exercise: What do low gears in a car do? | Give more turning force to start and climb | Make the car go as fast as it possibly can | Stop the car from rolling back | Save the most fuel on the motorway
exercise: Why do many electric cars need only one gear? | Their motors work well at all speeds | They are not allowed to have gears | They never go up hills | Gears would make them too heavy to move
exercise: What does a catalytic converter in the exhaust do? | Changes some poisonous gases into less harmful ones | Makes the car louder | Turns the exhaust into petrol again | Cools the engine with water
exercise: Why must an engine never run in a closed garage? | Exhaust fumes include invisible carbon monoxide | The garage door will melt | The engine will run out of air and explode | The car will slowly roll away all by itself
exercise: Where is all the petrol kept at a petrol station? | In big tanks underground | Inside the pumps themselves, one each | In the big fridge in the station shop | In a tanker parked on the roof
exercise: At the pump, where should children be? | In the car or away from the pumps | Holding the nozzle to help the grown-up | Playing in between the pumps out of the way | On a phone right next to the pump
exercise: Why do all the dashboard lights flash on when the car starts? | To show the bulbs work | Because everything in the car is broken | To wake the sleepy driver up | To warm up the screen on cold days
exercise: How does a small steering wheel turn the big front wheels? | Through a rod and gears, often helped by power steering | Through ropes tied to the tyres | The driver's feet push the wheels | The wheels follow the driver's eyes
exercise: What do a car's indicators show? | Which way the car is going to turn | How fast the car is going right now | That the driver is feeling happy today | That the car is running low on petrol
exercise: Why are reversing lights white? | So people behind know the car is about to move back | So the driver can read a map | So the driver can see the boot open | Because white bulbs are cheapest and last the longest
exercise: What does a mechanic plug a computer into a car for? | To read faults the car's sensors found | To charge the computer | To play music through the car's own speakers | To change the colour of the paint
exercise: In the UK, which cars need the yearly MOT test? | Cars older than three years | Only brand-new cars | Only red cars | Only cars with a mechanic inside
exercise: How does a car know it's raining? | A sensor notices raindrops changing the light on the glass | The driver presses a rain button | It hears the thunder | The tyres feel wet
exercise: Why aren't tyres smooth? | Grooves push water away so the rubber grips | Grooves make the tyres lighter and so faster | Smooth tyres are too expensive | Grooves make a nice noise
exercise: What is the UK minimum tread depth for car tyres? | 1.6 millimetres | 16 centimetres all round | About 1 metre deep | 0 millimetres, any tyre is fine
exercise: There are 6 cars in the car park. How many wheels touch the ground? | 24 | 18 | 12 | 30
test: my uncle says when the oil light comes on just ignore it till next week. good idea? | No, warning lights mean something should be checked soon | Yes, the lights are only decoration | Yes, they always come on for no reason at all, every single week | No, you should break the bulb
test: In a cartoon a kid crawls under a car lifted on a ramp to look. What's the real rule? | Never go under a raised car | It's fine if the engine is off | It's fine with a torch | It's fine if you're quick
test: Jake reckons the car moves because the engine pushes the road. Right? | No, burning fuel pushes pistons that turn the wheels | Yes, the engine has a long leg | Yes, the exhaust pushes hard on the road behind and shoves the car on | No, the steering wheel moves it
test: wipers came on by themselves, is the car haunted lol? | No, a rain sensor switched them on | Yes, cars can't do that alone | Yes, the radio did it | No, the driver's foot pressed them
test: What would happen on a wet road with tyres worn smooth? | The car would grip badly and take longer to stop | It would grip better than new tyres | Nothing, tread doesn't matter | The tyres would fill up with rainwater and the car would float away
test: Why do bikes and cars both have gears? | Low gears help start and climb, high gears go fast easily | To make the chain longer | To make them look complicated | Gears are only for racing
test: shut up and get out the car, I'm doing the petrol. Where do you wait? | Away from the pumps, or in the car | Next to the nozzle to help | Under the pump, where it's cool in the shade | On your phone by the pump
test: A car in front has a white light on at the back. What does it mean? | It is about to reverse | It is about to brake hard | It is parked there for ever | It wants to turn left next
test: Which is true about a car engine? | Many small explosions happen inside it every second | It runs on water from the windscreen washer bottle | It is cold inside while running | It only works when it rains
test: Some cars drip water from the exhaust pipe on a cold morning. Is the car broken? | Usually not; burning fuel makes water vapour that cools to drops | Yes, the engine is full of rain | Yes, the exhaust is leaking petrol | No, someone poured water in it
test: Leo wants to stand behind the car to wave while Dad starts it. What's the rule? | Never stand behind a car with its engine running | It's fine if you wave a lot | It's fine if the car is small and the driver is your own dad | Only stand in front of it
exam: Identify the principal function of the low gears of a motor vehicle. | The provision of greater turning force for starting and climbing | The attainment of the highest possible road speed | The reduction of fuel consumption on motorways | The prevention of the vehicle rolling backwards
exam: It is given that all warning lamps of a dashboard illuminate momentarily upon starting. State the reason therefor. | To demonstrate that the lamps are functional | To indicate that every system of the vehicle has failed | To summon the attention of a mechanic | To heat the instrument panel
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the purpose of the tread grooves of a tyre? | They displace water so that the rubber may grip the road | They reduce the mass of the tyre | They generate an agreeable sound | They prevent the tyre from rotating
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the operation of an engine within an enclosed garage. | It is dangerous owing to invisible carbon monoxide | It is safe provided the lights are on | It is required for the engine to warm | It is harmless for vehicles with diesel engines of recent manufacture
